{
  "image_name": "2bb285f2fe015e3b01d9ae7f92201135.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how endoplasmic reticulum–associated degradation (ERAD) identifies and removes abnormal proteins from the ER membrane system. Across the diagram, newly synthesized proteins enter the ER, where they may fold correctly or become misfolded, unassembled, or unstable. These defective proteins are first recognized in a substrate-specific targeting step, apparently by distinct ERAD E3 ligase complexes such as HRD1 or other ERAD E3 assemblies.\n\nOnce selected, the substrate is brought to the membrane complex and begins moving out of the ER toward the cytoplasm, although one panel notes that the exact “dislocation” route may vary among ERAD pathways. During this step, the substrate is tagged with chains of ubiquitin by E2 and E3 enzymes. The ATPase Cdc48 then extracts the ubiquitinated protein from the membrane or lumenal interface into the cytoplasm.\n\nAfter extraction, the protein undergoes remodeling with help from ubiquitin-binding proteins, deubiquitylating enzymes, and chaperones/transport factors. It is then delivered to the 26S proteasome for degradation. An inset emphasizes that ERAD E3 complexes recognize specific degradation signals, or degrons, exposed on different substrates. Overall, the figure highlights a common quality-control pathway: recognition, ubiquitination, extraction, remodeling, and proteasomal destruction.",
